utely intentional on emerald's  behalf she wanted people that you felt  warmly towards and comfortable with i  don't think those  those scenes work if if you have  expectations  that somebody is going to be not a nice  guy i think if you see the guy who  ordinarily plays the villain um the  impact isn't really there and that was  particularly true for al monroe and i  think what's so powerful about chris  in that role and also in the writing is  that this is not somebody who thinks of  himself as being a bad guy  he doesn't want to go upstairs he  doesn't really want  you know he says to her like i don't  want to do anything i love my girlfriend  as an audience we we believed in these  guys until we saw  those scenes turn what was the  difference between cassie using red  versus black pen for the tally marks in  her notebook  that is for me and emerald to know and  the audience not to know for a minute  there ryan and cassie appear to have a  pretty great relationship do you think  cassie really loved ryan  yeah 100 i think she did i think it's  the easier path i think she really did  love him and i think  it's so it's sort of more heartbreaking  if the audience sees the life that she  could have had  you know if she chooses to but there's  just no way of getting past what she  discovers about him  so it's clear-cut from there the ending  is absolutely bonkers and it's  bittersweet for viewers what was your  reaction when you first read the script  it is bonkers the whole thing in that  you are constantly kind of wrong-footed  and i love that about it i think it's so  exciting refreshing to read a script  where  you don't know what's coming and you  keep having your expectations thrown off  and i definitely wasn't expecting the  ending but  i felt so strongly like emerald did that  it was important to tell the truth  and that that felt like the honest  ending to the film perhaps not the one  that we all  might have wanted um but that was the  truthful way to end the